WEBVTT


00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:15.596
That was one hell of a bang.


00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:18.710
You ain't seen nothing yet.


00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:23.756
That is so gross.


00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.148
- He's so old.
- I know.


00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:28.952
She could be, like,
his granddaughter.


00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.672
That sucked.


00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.637
Oh, my God! I don't
believe it, you're...


00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.757
...Sean Connery!


00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.677
Oh, my God!


00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.677
Hi, Sean.


00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.519
- Oh, Sean Connery.
- Thank you.


00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.278
<i>Please make a right turn.</i>


00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.911
<i>- Make a right turn.
- I heard you the first time.</i>


00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.309
<i>You are now leaving
Beverly Hills.</i>


00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.631
<i>Are you sure you want to
be in this neighbourhood?</i>


00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.760
<i>You have arrived
at your destination.</i>


00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.317
<i>Pride and Prejudice.</i>


00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:58.351
"I remember hearing you
once say, Mr Darcy...


00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:01.594
"that you hardly
ever forgave...


00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.748
"and that your resentment, once
created, was, kind of, unappeasable.


00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:12.238
"I guess you're very cautious
to its being created and shit. "


00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.230
My daughter Amanda put
my name on the VIP list.


00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.318
But we don't
believe in VIP lists.


00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:28.595
Do you know who I am?


00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.759
- Do any of us really know who we are?
- Just give me a ticket.


00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:35.794
"Like, wow.


00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.115
"And you never, like, allow
yourself to be blinded by prejudice?"


00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:44.990
- "I hope not. "
- "Cool. "


00:03:45.400 --> 00:03:47.630
"May I ask to what
these questions tend?"


00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.274
Isn't that Jefferson Steel?


00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:53.119
I thought he was dead.


00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.477
"I do not get you at all, man.


00:03:57.760 --> 00:04:03.118
"I hear such different account
of you that it's freaking me out.


00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:05.713
"Exceedingly. "


00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.388
Mr Steel?


00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:16.557
I don't want you to think I'm
the kind of desperate actor.


00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:21.037
...who goes around giving people
his resume, but here's my resume.


00:04:21.520 --> 00:04:22.748
Hey, Dad.


00:04:23.960 --> 00:04:25.837
So, what did you think?


00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:30.079
I loved it.


00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.554
- Really?
- Really.


00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:42.509
- So you really liked it?
- I hated it.


00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.629
What?


00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:48.231
I think the director is a
pretentious, talentless pervert.


00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.197
Oh, that's just great.


00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.557
My first time doing a
classic, playing the lead...


00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.877
...and you're objecting to a
small amount of justifiable nudity?


00:04:58.000 --> 00:04:59.353
Justifiable?


00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.676
- Forget it.
- I don't get it.


00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.115
I've given you everything
I know how to give you...


00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:05.912
...and you've thrown it
right back in my face.


00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.352
I don't want your contacts,
I don't want a fancy car...


00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.710
I don't want a house
in Beverly Hills...


00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:17.231
...and when I want some career advice,
I'll ask someone who's got a career.


00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:33.716
<i>I'm ready for my close-up.</i>


00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:37.352
<i>Where is it, Dad?</i>


00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:41.474
<i>You never know what there
could be in the desert...</i>


00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.034
<i>...poisonous spiders,
caterpillars...</i>


00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:50.911
<i>Hi, Dad. Gotcha!</i>


00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:06.991
<i>Hi, this is Amanda Blacke.</i>


00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:10.157
<i>I can't take your call right
now, so please leave a message.</i>


00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:13.397
<i>Unless you're my father,
in which case, forget it.</i>


00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:18.395
That's very funny. Notice
that I'm not laughing.


00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:22.838
<i>Now who the hell is Amanda
Blacke? You're Amanda Steel.</i>


00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:25.599
<i>This is your father,
call me some time.</i>


00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:45.959
Ring, damn you!


00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.558
<i>Charlie Rosenberg.
Charlie?</i>


00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:01.550
Jefferson, can I call you
back? I'm in a meeting.


00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:02.795
<i>No, you're not, Charlie.</i>


00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:05.514
Really, now is not a
good... Sorry, Mr Rosenberg.


00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:10.714
<i>Hey, Jefferson! Ultimate
Finality 4, what a movie!</i>


00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.513
One of the worst films
I ever made in my life.


00:07:13.640 --> 00:07:15.312
The motorcycle was cool.


00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:19.069
And since then,
Charlie, nothing.


00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:20.599
Where are the offers, Charlie?


00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.235
That's the problem
with being a major star.


00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.796
There just aren't enough projects
big enough for Jefferson Steel.


00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:30.832
You mean if I was less of
a star, I could get a job?


00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:33.235
- Exactly.
- You're full of shit.


00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.998
Yes, I am.


00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.511
Charlie, if you don't get me
a decent part, I'm out of here.


00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:40.993
Are you threatening me?


00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:42.951
- Isn't it obvious?
- Of course.


00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:48.279
- Are you saying I can't do threatening?
- Nadine? Nadine, back me up here.


00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:51.039
Tell Jefferson how good
he is at threatening.


00:07:51.160 --> 00:07:55.199
Oh, you're terrifically threatening.
In fact, you're almost scary.


00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:04.752
You've got 24 hours to get me a job
or I'm going to fire your sorry ass.


00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.108
- Very good. Very, very good.
- It's really very good.


00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:24.392
This is hopeless. Who am I kidding?
Jefferson's gonna fire me...


00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.114
...my wife's gonna divorce
me, she'll take the house...


00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:30.073
...she'll get the dogs,
she'll get the kids...


00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:34.997
- Silver lining, huh?
- It's not gonna happen, Charlie.


00:08:35.120 --> 00:08:39.113
Your wife already divorced
you and your kids have kids.


00:08:39.840 --> 00:08:40.989
Nadine.


00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:44.399
This is it.


00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:47.318
This is it.


00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.029
- Shakespeare?
- Yeah.


00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.837
A benefit to save a community
theatre in England? In the sticks?


00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:00.229
Yeah!


00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:04.197
It's not even professional.
Jefferson will never go for this.


00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.834
Sure he will, he's desperate.


00:09:06.960 --> 00:09:10.635
And besides, he'll be 6,000 miles
away before he figures it out.


00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:16.795
- This better be good, Charlie.
- It's the best part there is, ever.


00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:18.558
What is it?


00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:21.631
<i>King Lear.</i>


00:09:21.760 --> 00:09:24.479
- By Shakespeare?
- That's the one.


00:09:24.600 --> 00:09:25.874
And guess where it is.


00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:27.831
Now, how in the hell
should I know, Charlie?


00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:28.949
England.


00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:32.868
It's your chance to reinvent yourself,
get Jefferson Steel back on the map.


00:09:33.000 --> 00:09:35.878
- By leaving the country?
- Everyone has done England.


00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.152
Nicole, Dustin, Gwyneth,
Spacey. And here's the kicker...


00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:45.792
...none of them have ever
done Shakespeare in Stratford.


00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.757
- It's a no-brainer. -
I don't know, Charlie.


00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:54.429
A man in my position, I have
to consider all the options.


00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:32.278
- We'll be landing in about 40
minutes, okay? Great, thanks.


00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.069
Excuse me. So sorry to
bother you, Mr Steel.


00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:40.431
No problem, honey.
Who shall I sign it to?


00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.472
The United Kingdom
Immigration Department.


00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:45.272
It's your landing card.


00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.753
I knew that.


00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.191
Hey, Tom Selleck! Hey!


00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.432
<i>We now go live to
Heathrow Airport.</i>


00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:09.118
<i>...for the Jefferson
Steel press conference.</i>


00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:11.879
<i>This is a pretty big departure
for your career, Mr Steel.</i>


00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:14.230
<i>I'm an actor. Theatre
is my first love.</i>


00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:17.238
<i>Mr Steele, what made
you say yes to this?</i>


00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.670
<i>Well, I get a lot of scripts sent
to me. I think this one stood out.</i>


00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:25.112
- How so?
- The writing showed real promise.


00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:30.470
Jefferson, can an American
action hero really be King Lear?


00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:35.635
"To be, or not to be,
that is the question. "


00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:37.193
<i>That's Hamlet.</i>


00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.436
Jefferson, can you
really save the theatre?


00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:43.913
I'll give it my best shot.


00:11:44.040 --> 00:11:45.837
But you'll be
acting with amateurs.


00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:51.159
You guys are a bit too hard on yourselves.
Some British actors are pretty damn good.


00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.594
<i>Now boarding at Gate 11.</i>


00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.871
Mr Steel! How do
you do, Mr Steel?


00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:06.599
I'm Dorothy Nettle. It's
an honour to meet you.


00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.109
Of course it is.
Where's the limo?


00:12:13.880 --> 00:12:18.351
It's not exactly a stretch limo,
but we can pick up Radio Ipswich.


00:12:18.480 --> 00:12:22.837
So, you just sit back and relax. It's
about a two-and-a-half hour drive.


00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.598
I'm sure I don't
have to say it...


00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.870
...but everyone's really looking
forward to working with you.


00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:29.753
There's been a real buzz in the air
since your agent told us you were coming.


00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:31.950
- As a matter of fact...
- No offence...


00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:34.992
...but I don't talk to the
driver. It's in my contract.


00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:37.190
Nice meeting you, though.


00:12:37.840 --> 00:12:38.955
Right.


00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:24.475
Oh!


00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.995
Welcome to Flint
Farm, Mr Steel.


00:13:30.240 --> 00:13:32.117
Don't mind Monty,
he's very friendly.


00:13:33.800 --> 00:13:36.872
That's good, because if
he bites me, I'll sue.


00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.349
Brrr.


00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:51.073
This is such a
privilege, Mr Steel.


00:13:51.200 --> 00:13:52.349
I know.


00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:58.711
And can I say, you are so much
younger than you look in your films.


00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:02.792
Not that you look old
in them, you look young.


00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:06.629
Very young, especially
in the old ones.


00:14:09.440 --> 00:14:10.919
Is there anything
you would like?


00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.112
A room with a bed, of
course. That would be nice.


00:14:14.240 --> 00:14:17.676
Of course. Yes, it's just
at the top of the stairs.


00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:19.791
After you, Mr Steel.


00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:28.319
It's just the first
door on the right, there.


00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:33.150
<i>Oh, I love all your films,
especially The Fugitive.</i>


00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:35.271
<i>I wasn't in The Fugitive.</i>


00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:38.517
- Are you sure?
- Quite sure.


00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:42.515
- Ow!
- Mind the beam.


00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:43.948
Mmm-hmm.


00:14:48.680 --> 00:14:51.831
I've given you
the master suite.


00:14:51.960 --> 00:14:55.999
Where do I go to?
You know, whiz.


00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.035
Take a pee.


00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.914
Yes, the facilities are down
the corridor and on the right.


00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:05.237
We thought you'd prefer
somewhere unpretentious.


00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.112
Well, you were wrong.


00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.916
He's a real charmer, isn't he?


00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:19.990
It's how these stars get into their
characters. It's what they call method acting.


00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.432
You see, King Lear
is really grumpy...


00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:27.996
...whereas if he was playing
Romeo, he'd be a real sweetie.


00:15:29.520 --> 00:15:33.991
Where is the minibar?
Where is the mini...


00:15:35.600 --> 00:15:37.830
There is no minibar!


00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.275
There is no minibar!


00:15:41.400 --> 00:15:45.678
- Are you going to be all right, Mary?
- Oh, we'll be fine.


00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:52.159
Could I have room service,
please? This is Jefferson Steel.


00:15:53.400 --> 00:15:54.515
Hello? Hello?


00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:57.518
<i>The number you have dialled
has not been recognised.</i>


00:16:08.760 --> 00:16:09.988
Oh!


00:16:10.960 --> 00:16:15.476
It's 4:01 and we
are not sleeping.


00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:39.239
Good doggy.


00:16:40.120 --> 00:16:42.236
Eat, enjoy.


00:16:45.840 --> 00:16:48.229
Now, where is that bathroom?


00:16:53.560 --> 00:16:59.192
Oh, why... Oh, Mr Steel,
this is a bit sudden.


00:16:59.320 --> 00:17:03.472
- I was just looking for the... I needed to... I...
- Yes?


00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:05.192
Forget it.


00:17:20.320 --> 00:17:21.514
Ow! Damn it.


00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:37.638
So, how do you think film stars like
their fried bread, Monty? Hmm? Hmm?


00:17:37.880 --> 00:17:41.509
Not too burnt. You're probably
right. Would you like this?


00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.914
So, what's he like?


00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:48.836
Well, he is very handsome.


00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:51.554
Good for his age,
well preserved.


00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:54.990
You are just jealous.


00:17:55.160 --> 00:17:57.310
Apparently, he's a sexaholic.


00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:01.708
I read it in a magazine. He's
insatiable. Anything in a skirt.


00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:03.679
- Really?
- Mmm.


00:18:06.120 --> 00:18:07.792
Oh! Damn it!


00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:26.038
- Morning, Mr
Steel. - Mmm-hmm.


00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:28.151
And what can I get you?


00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.478
I think a suite at the Four
Seasons would be nice...


00:18:31.600 --> 00:18:34.990
...and possibly a
new pair of boots.


00:18:36.160 --> 00:18:38.720
The full English?


00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:44.879
I want a glass of guava juice, egg
white frittata with brocollini...


00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:48.709
...rice, cheese and
peppers, and a non-fat latte.


00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:51.754
And toast?


00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:09.717
- How's King Lear this morning?
- I think he might be missing sex.


00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:10.795
Still grumpy?


00:19:10.920 --> 00:19:11.875
Not as grumpy as
when he finds out.


00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:15.913
I haven't got any guavalini,
frittata juice or egg-white lattes.


00:19:17.560 --> 00:19:19.471
No, don't tell him.


00:19:20.560 --> 00:19:22.437
Good morning, Mr Steel.


00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.710
- It's you again.
- Dorothy.


00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:28.034
Dorothy.


00:19:28.600 --> 00:19:33.116
Would you tell your people that I'm
just not happy with the accommodations.


00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:37.234
Mary, apparently Mr Steel isn't
happy with the accommodation.


00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.912
Well, I'm very sorry, but
he's in the best room I've got.


00:19:41.040 --> 00:19:42.792
The management is
very sorry, Mr Steel...


00:19:42.920 --> 00:19:45.957
...but apparently you are
occupying the best room available.


00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:49.997
You are joking, right?


00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.358
Are you joking, Mary?
No, she's not joking.


00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:54.709
Oh.


00:19:54.840 --> 00:19:58.753
Well, we have to set off, Mr Steel.
Rehearsal begins in five minutes.


00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:00.632
Tell the car to wait.


00:20:05.560 --> 00:20:09.189
- Where's the car?
- Oh, it's only a little walk from here.


00:20:09.320 --> 00:20:11.072
We don't walk in LA.


00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:19.598
I don't want to be unreasonable, but
I do have a few basic requirements.


00:20:19.720 --> 00:20:20.994
Of course.


00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.995
I want a warm, warm trailer...


00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:30.999
...with a minibar,
satellite TV and a hot tub.


00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:34.230
- Is that all?
- No.


00:20:34.360 --> 00:20:40.117
<i>I also get a personal trainer and
a spiritual healer on call 24l7.</i>


00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:43.471
I'll see what I can
do. Anything else?


00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.434
Yes. Can you do
something about this...


00:20:49.240 --> 00:20:50.559
...weather?


00:20:52.960 --> 00:20:54.757
Apparently not.


00:20:57.640 --> 00:20:59.790
Where the hell is this place?


00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:05.390
- We're here. This is it.
- This is what?


00:21:05.520 --> 00:21:07.556
Oh, my God!


00:21:09.680 --> 00:21:12.353
You're here,
you're really here!


00:21:12.480 --> 00:21:15.916
- This is my daughter, Verity.
- I knew he'd come. I told you he'd come.


00:21:16.040 --> 00:21:18.554
Darling, you shouldn't be outside
without a coat, you'll catch a cold.


00:21:18.680 --> 00:21:21.114
Oh, please, Mum. Like,
I'm sure Jefferson Steel.


00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:22.912
...is interested in
my health problems.


00:21:26.600 --> 00:21:27.555
Oh, my God!


00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:28.999
Verity has a lot of allergies
and she's prone to...


00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:31.759
She's right. I'm not
really interested.


00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:35.350
You really are getting
into the part, aren't you?


00:21:36.320 --> 00:21:39.198
Ladies and gentlemen,
Mr Jefferson Steel.


00:21:48.920 --> 00:21:51.673
There is nothing more
humbling to an actor...


00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:54.758
...than to receive the
approbation of his fellow actors.


00:21:56.000 --> 00:22:00.676
May I introduce Frank Dobbins, who
is playing the Earl of Gloucester.


00:22:00.800 --> 00:22:01.755
Wotcha!


00:22:01.880 --> 00:22:04.348
- Janine Jarvis, who is playing Regan.
- Hiya.


00:22:04.480 --> 00:22:05.708
Hey.


00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:09.071
- Kevin Patel, who is Edmund.
- Respect, man.


00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:12.553
Rupert Twisk, who is
playing the Duke of Cornwall.


00:22:12.680 --> 00:22:14.636
Am I? Oh, brilliant.


00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:17.439
- And Edgar.
- Oh, shit.


00:22:17.560 --> 00:22:19.710
Verity Nettle is your
loving daughter Cordelia.


00:22:19.840 --> 00:22:21.432
Hello, again.


00:22:21.640 --> 00:22:26.316
And, of course, Nigel Dewberry, who
will be giving us his Earl of Kent.


00:22:26.440 --> 00:22:29.159
The privilege is all mine.


00:22:30.040 --> 00:22:32.952
- Could I speak to you for just a moment?
- Yes, of course.


00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:34.798
I...


00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:41.393
I don't recognise
any of these people.


00:22:41.520 --> 00:22:45.911
I mean, where's Judi Dench?
Where's Kenny Branagh?


00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:50.397
- Just throw in one of the Redgraves.
- All otherwise engaged, I'm afraid.


00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:56.278
- I need to talk to the director.
- You are.


00:22:57.880 --> 00:23:00.030
- You're the director?
- Yes, I am.


00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:02.913
- I thought you were the driver.
- Well, I'm that too.


00:23:03.040 --> 00:23:04.678
She's also playing the fool.


00:23:04.800 --> 00:23:09.316
But as director, I'd like to get
cracking with the rehearsal, okay?


00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:13.151
- This dump isn't the rehearsal hall, is it?
- No, of course not.


00:23:13.280 --> 00:23:14.713
Oh, thank God.


00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.910
This is our theatre.


00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.310
- Here?
- Well, actually, no.


00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:27.113
Down there.


00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:33.913
A poor thing, but our own.


00:23:35.640 --> 00:23:39.235
What has the Royal
Shakespeare Company come to?


00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:42.355
The Royal Shakespeare Company?


00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:48.668
<i>My agent said I was going
to do Lear at Stratford.</i>


00:23:48.800 --> 00:23:52.793
- And so you are.
- Yes. We are The Stratford Players.


00:23:54.240 --> 00:23:58.597
- Where Shakespeare was born, right?
- Well, no, not... Not exactly.


00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:03.111
You see, this is Stratford,
it just isn't upon-Avon.


00:24:03.240 --> 00:24:07.028
- This is Stratford St John.
- Sinjun.


00:24:07.160 --> 00:24:12.188
In Suffolk. We're just a
small, amateur theatre group.


00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.476
Charlie!


00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:31.116
I'm gonna kill
Charlie Rosenberg...


00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.073
...and then I'm gonna have him revived
and then I'm gonna kill him again.


00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:35.918
I did explain all
this in the letter.


00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:37.393
The council have
cut our funding.


00:24:37.520 --> 00:24:39.431
...and unless we can raise the
money, we're gonna have to close.


00:24:39.560 --> 00:24:42.791
A big name means big
sponsorship and big publicity.


00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.275
You're going to
save our theatre.


00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:48.639
Sorry to disappoint
you, young lady...


00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:52.355
...but I'm not in the
business of doing charity gigs.


00:24:52.480 --> 00:24:53.754
But you promised.


00:24:53.880 --> 00:24:56.678
It was on the news. You
were really inspiring.


00:24:56.800 --> 00:24:59.598
"Theatre's in my blood" and all
that stuff. It was jolly moving.


00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:01.631
I was acting, you idiot.


00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:07.077
What the hell is the matter
with this thing? I can't get LA.


00:25:07.200 --> 00:25:08.679
You can't even
get Luton, mate.


00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:11.598
There's no signal round
here. We're in a black hole.


00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:14.280
There's a public telephone
thingy down the lane by the pub.


00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:15.628
But you'll need
some coins, though.


00:25:15.760 --> 00:25:18.228
Verity, go and get the
"Save the Theatre" swear box.


00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:22.271
I'm not here to save
your fucking theatre.


00:25:34.160 --> 00:25:37.755
<i>Charlie!
- Jesus, Jefferson, what's going on?</i>


00:25:37.880 --> 00:25:40.997
I'm on a pig farm.


00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:44.519
<i>Jesus, Jefferson, it's
2:00 in the morning.</i>


00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:50.078
You booked me into the
wrong Stratford, you idiot!


00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:53.678
This is
Stratford-on-Pig Farm.


00:25:53.800 --> 00:25:56.598
Yeah, yeah, I'm sorry
about that, I really am...


00:25:56.960 --> 00:25:58.757
...but you're getting
great coverage.


00:25:58.880 --> 00:26:00.359
Get me out of here!


00:26:00.480 --> 00:26:02.357
<i>- Now!
- I can't do that, Jefferson.</i>


00:26:02.480 --> 00:26:04.948
The story's too big.
It would finish you.


00:26:05.080 --> 00:26:07.435
So, you're telling me
there's something worse.


00:26:07.560 --> 00:26:11.348
...than working on a pig
farm with a bunch of amateurs?


00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:18.830
A bunch of
amateurs is waiting.


00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:21.352
Shall we head back?


00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:27.192
It's an easy mistake
to make, Mr Steel.


00:26:27.600 --> 00:26:30.433
There are several
Stratfords in Britain.


00:26:30.560 --> 00:26:33.757
Stratford-upon-Avon, of
course. That's in Warwickshire.


00:26:33.880 --> 00:26:36.314
Then there's Stratford
in East London.


00:26:36.440 --> 00:26:37.475
Stratford Tony in Wiltshire.


00:26:37.600 --> 00:26:39.875
Yes, thank you both. I think
Mr Steel gets the picture.


00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:42.719
Stratford St Agnes
in Somerset...


00:26:42.840 --> 00:26:46.116
Stratford St Andrew,
Stratford St Mary...


00:26:48.000 --> 00:26:49.399
Excuse me...


00:26:51.720 --> 00:26:53.153
Frank.
Frank.


00:26:54.640 --> 00:26:56.915
Would you do me
a favour, Frank?


00:26:58.040 --> 00:26:59.632
Shut the fuck up.


00:27:02.800 --> 00:27:06.236
- Oh, sorry. Sorry I'm late, Dot.
- No, you're okay.


00:27:06.360 --> 00:27:09.352
Just had to go into town,
get some guava juice.


00:27:14.760 --> 00:27:19.550
Right! So, Mr Steel,
shall we give it a whirl?


00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:22.353
I wonder, Dorothy, if
we could have a word?


00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:26.792
We've sort of had a clever idea for
when Gloucester has his eyes gouged out.


00:27:26.920 --> 00:27:29.673
Well, maybe later? Okay.


00:27:29.800 --> 00:27:32.553
Right, everyone, we're going to
pick it up from Act 1, Scene 1...


00:27:32.680 --> 00:27:34.910
...just after
Gloucester's exit.


00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:37.395
Everyone should probably
remember where they were.


00:27:37.520 --> 00:27:40.273
Cornwall and Albany
were this side...


00:27:40.400 --> 00:27:43.631
...and Danny and Tai, I think we had the
two guards at the back, if you remember.


00:27:43.760 --> 00:27:44.829
Okay!


00:27:46.080 --> 00:27:48.275
Okay, here we go.


00:27:48.400 --> 00:27:52.598
Let's take it from the top
of Lear's first big speech.


00:27:53.760 --> 00:27:55.796
In your own time, Mr Steel.


00:27:57.800 --> 00:27:59.711
"Give me the map there. "


00:28:03.960 --> 00:28:05.473
You see, that's a problem.


00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:10.230
I mean, right away I ask, "Give
me the map", nobody gives me a map.


00:28:10.360 --> 00:28:12.920
The whole thing's
turning to shit here.


00:28:13.040 --> 00:28:14.519
Couldn't you just
pretend for now?


00:28:14.640 --> 00:28:16.312
It's called acting.


00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:21.798
"Give me the map.


00:28:23.640 --> 00:28:27.189
"Know that we have divided
in three our kingdom...


00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:30.517
"and 'tis our fast intent to shake
all cares and business from our age...


00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:34.599
"conferring them on younger strengths,
while we, Unburthen'd, crawl towards death.


00:28:35.240 --> 00:28:38.516
"Interest of territory, cares
of state... " Etcetera, etcetera.


00:28:38.640 --> 00:28:42.155
"Where nature doth
with merit challenge. "


00:28:42.320 --> 00:28:44.311
"Gon... Gon... Gon...


00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:47.516
- "Gon... "
- "Goneril. "


00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:52.953
That's me, and she's the one who says
she loves you, but doesn't really.


00:28:53.080 --> 00:28:55.878
Though that's obviously only
the character, not in real life.


00:28:56.000 --> 00:28:59.276
That's not to say that
I don't like you in...


00:29:00.840 --> 00:29:03.229
No, but that's not...


00:29:04.040 --> 00:29:05.234
Your point is?


00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:09.558
The point is that Mary's
character is pronounced "Goneril".


00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:11.159
Well, you do know
the play, Mr Steel.


00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:12.508
Oh, yes, I know it.


00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:17.077
It's about a man whose three
daughters drive him nuts.


00:29:17.920 --> 00:29:19.638
And you've read it?


00:29:20.920 --> 00:29:25.277
I like to start
with a blank canvas.


00:29:27.720 --> 00:29:29.438
You haven't read it.


00:29:37.360 --> 00:29:40.477
I'm sorry I had to put my foot
down, but it's the law here.


00:29:40.600 --> 00:29:43.717
You're not allowed to
smoke in a public building.


00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:49.875
- It's hardly even a barn.
- Besides, Verity's allergic to the smoke.


00:29:54.800 --> 00:29:57.917
It was her idea to write
you in the first place.


00:29:59.320 --> 00:30:00.958
We were desperate.


00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:03.509
Thanks.


00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:06.748
She seems to think
you're wonderful.


00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:11.158
- She's a very bright kid.
- She is.


00:30:12.720 --> 00:30:14.631
She's even read the play.


00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:19.718
You know what happened to the last director
who thought they were smarter than me?


00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:22.388
They won an Oscar?


00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:25.439
That's funny.


00:30:26.560 --> 00:30:28.949
No wonder you're
playing the fool.


00:30:30.280 --> 00:30:31.998
Shall we go back?


00:30:32.120 --> 00:30:33.792
Do I have a choice?


00:30:34.360 --> 00:30:35.998
That's the spirit.


00:30:38.400 --> 00:30:39.958
"Lead on, Macduff. "


00:30:42.880 --> 00:30:46.509
"I here disclaim all
my paternal care...


00:30:46.640 --> 00:30:49.438
"Propinquity and
property of blood,"


00:30:49.560 --> 00:30:53.872
"And as a stranger
to my heart and me.


00:30:54.000 --> 00:30:57.231
"Hold thee, from
this, forever. "


00:30:57.760 --> 00:31:01.275
Give it a rest, Nigel. Can you get
us a packet of pork scratchings?


00:31:01.400 --> 00:31:03.550
Well, I'm sorry,
but it's not fair.


00:31:04.360 --> 00:31:07.636
I should be Lear. It's
every actor's dream.


00:31:07.760 --> 00:31:10.593
You is a lawyer,
Nige. Accept it, yeah?


00:31:10.720 --> 00:31:13.757
But, I mean, he's so
stilted and wooden.


00:31:13.880 --> 00:31:16.678
I mean, what has Jefferson
Steel got that I haven't?


00:31:16.800 --> 00:31:21.396
Good looks, money, fame, a Beverly
Hills mansion... How long have you got?


00:31:21.520 --> 00:31:22.669
Well, where is he, then?


00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:24.119
He's having some extra
rehearsals with Dorothy.


00:31:24.240 --> 00:31:25.912
He'll need all the
rehearsals he can get.


00:31:26.040 --> 00:31:29.157
...because let's face it,
he's no Laurence Olivier.


00:31:29.600 --> 00:31:34.435
In fact, he might even be the first actor in
history that's actually too old to play Lear.


00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:38.198
- He'd make a damn good Yorick.
- Who's Yorick?


00:31:38.320 --> 00:31:40.356
<i>The skull in Hamlet.</i>


00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:42.148
- Harsh.
- But fair.


00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:45.431
That is unfair. I think
you're all being very cruel.


00:31:45.560 --> 00:31:48.438
It is early days, and we
shouldn't make any rash judgements.


00:31:48.560 --> 00:31:51.472
...because he is
totally fantastic.


00:31:53.080 --> 00:31:54.991
Mary, you're dribbling.


00:31:55.400 --> 00:31:57.072
Thank you.


00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:02.916
Maybe you should think
more about your motivation.


00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:07.192
- How do you like Stratford, Mr Steel?
- It's not what I expected.


00:32:07.600 --> 00:32:09.591
<i>How are you coping
with King Lear?</i>


00:32:09.720 --> 00:32:11.995
Your daughter, Amanda,
thinks it's perfect casting.


00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:14.588
- You talked to my daughter?
- Oh, yeah. She said, and I quote...


00:32:14.720 --> 00:32:16.597
"Lear is an arrogant egomaniac
and a lousy father... "


00:32:16.720 --> 00:32:19.473
Excuse me, gentlemen. Mr Steel has had
a long and exhausting day in rehearsal...


00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:21.192
...and now has an
important meeting to attend.


00:32:24.200 --> 00:32:25.713
Thank you very much.


00:32:25.840 --> 00:32:28.434
No, no, no, we really have
got a meeting. With a sponsor.


00:32:28.560 --> 00:32:33.554
He paid for your flights and all the
production costs, so please be nice to him.


00:32:33.680 --> 00:32:34.749
Yeah.


00:32:34.880 --> 00:32:41.228
Jefferson, this is Mike Bell,
managing director of Anglian Breweries.


00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:43.510
- Pleased to meet you, Jeff.
- Thank you.


00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:45.551
This is Lauren, my wife.


00:32:47.600 --> 00:32:51.149
Hello. You're
obviously not in beer.


00:32:51.280 --> 00:32:53.316
No, Lauren is a
fitness instructor.


00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:55.396
I'm a big fan of
yours, Mr Steel.


00:32:55.520 --> 00:32:57.829
Well, that makes two of us.


00:33:01.120 --> 00:33:04.795
Jefferson was asking if there was a way
to tie the show in with your product.


00:33:04.920 --> 00:33:06.194
- I was?
- Mmm-hmm.


00:33:06.320 --> 00:33:11.189
I'm ahead of you, Jeff.
May I introduce King Beer.


00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:18.035
- It's like King Lear, you see?
- I get it.


00:33:18.160 --> 00:33:23.234
Go on, get it down you. The
finest ale this side of Royston.


00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:26.195
Right.


00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:33.914
Jetlag.


00:33:36.520 --> 00:33:38.511
Do you deliberately set
out to offend people?


00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:40.870
No, it just comes natural.


00:33:42.400 --> 00:33:44.118
Hello.
- Hello.


00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:46.908
Except when they're
pretty blondes.


00:33:57.760 --> 00:34:00.194
It's so long.


00:34:00.320 --> 00:34:04.950
Here we are, Mr Steel.
Everything you asked for.


00:34:07.280 --> 00:34:11.592
I don't have time for breakfast.
I've got to find Dorothy.


00:34:16.400 --> 00:34:20.109
Well, here's a fancy,
Beverly-Hills-style breakfast for you.


00:34:23.840 --> 00:34:26.308
Don't you start
getting picky on me.


00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:30.589
Next thing, you'll be suggesting
that your people talk to my people.


00:34:30.720 --> 00:34:32.915
Well, I don't have any people.


00:34:57.200 --> 00:34:58.679
Dorothy.


00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:01.477
Jefferson. Good morning.


00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.477
I have some issues.


00:35:04.120 --> 00:35:05.758
- Issues?
- Mmm-hmm.


00:35:07.680 --> 00:35:10.478
To be honest, I think
this needs a rewrite.


00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:13.991
- But this is Shakespeare.
- Well, nobody's perfect.


00:35:15.160 --> 00:35:17.993
I say we cut two acts,
give it a happy ending.


00:35:18.120 --> 00:35:20.350
We're going to do
the play as written.


00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:22.118
You never heard
of improvisation?


00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:24.800
You can't improvise
Shakespeare.


00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:26.558
How would we do the
scene on the heath?


00:35:26.680 --> 00:35:29.319
We're not doing the
scene on the heath.


00:35:30.080 --> 00:35:31.672
I don't do crazy.


00:35:32.800 --> 00:35:36.759
Do I detect a hint of panic?
All those words, all that acting.


00:35:36.880 --> 00:35:38.393
I don't panic.


00:35:38.520 --> 00:35:41.432
Where is everybody? Why
aren't we rehearsing?


00:35:41.760 --> 00:35:44.593
Well, it's a weekday.
Everyone's got proper jobs.


00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:46.517
We'll rehearse
again this evening.


00:35:46.640 --> 00:35:52.351
Well, what am I gonna do all day? I'd be
in my trailer, but I don't have a trailer.


00:35:52.480 --> 00:35:55.790
Well, you could always help
out here at the library.


00:35:56.120 --> 00:35:57.235
Mmm.


00:35:58.240 --> 00:36:00.276
I think I'll take a stroll.


00:36:02.200 --> 00:36:03.269
Okay.


00:36:15.720 --> 00:36:18.917
Jeff, it's you! Mate,
it's you. It's you, Jeff!


00:36:19.800 --> 00:36:21.472
Morning, Jefferson!


00:36:22.200 --> 00:36:24.998
Morning. Hello.


00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:34.634
- Oh, hang on a minute.
- Oh!


00:36:34.800 --> 00:36:36.028
Yoo-hoo!


00:36:37.720 --> 00:36:38.835
Hello?


00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:43.390
There goes our
local celebrity.


00:36:44.160 --> 00:36:46.594
Trying to act inconspicuous.


00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:47.709
Hi.


00:36:47.840 --> 00:36:49.512
Singularly failing.


00:36:56.720 --> 00:36:58.438
No pictures, please.


00:37:13.400 --> 00:37:16.517
I've just taken a
tour of downtown.


00:37:17.840 --> 00:37:20.673
I've killed three
minutes. What do I do now?


00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:25.829
Young man? Have you got any
books on herbaceous borders?


00:37:25.960 --> 00:37:27.996
Do I look like a librarian?


00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:33.079
- No.
- Are you saying I can't do librarian?


00:37:35.760 --> 00:37:39.196
Now, let me see. "Herbaceous.
" That would be under "H".


00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:43.072
He seems very
nice. Is he taken?


00:37:47.240 --> 00:37:48.389
"Cornwall and Albany...


00:37:48.520 --> 00:37:51.876
"With my two daughters'
dowers digest the third.


00:37:52.000 --> 00:37:56.278
"I do invest you jointly with my
power, Pre-eminence and all... "


00:37:56.400 --> 00:38:00.234
You have to admit, he
has got such charisma.


00:38:00.360 --> 00:38:02.828
Oh, God. Well,
where is it, then?


00:38:02.960 --> 00:38:04.598
"... of a hundred knights... "


00:38:04.720 --> 00:38:08.269
He's getting to know the character,
Janine, it doesn't happen overnight.


00:38:08.400 --> 00:38:12.996
He's the same with me at home,
tip-toeing around me, being polite.


00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:17.591
Well, not polite, exactly, but you know...
You know what I mean. Feeling his way.


00:38:17.720 --> 00:38:19.472
You wish.


00:38:21.000 --> 00:38:23.355
Tenner says he'll jack
it in within the week.


00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:25.755
- Twenty says he won't last three days.
- You're on.


00:38:25.880 --> 00:38:27.359
- Quiet! Shh!
- Sorry.


00:38:27.480 --> 00:38:31.917
"Royal Lear, Whom I have
ever honour'd as my king,"


00:38:32.200 --> 00:38:36.113
"Loved as my father,
as my master follow'd...


00:38:36.240 --> 00:38:39.437
"As my great patron thought
on in my prayers... "


00:38:40.480 --> 00:38:43.916
Why does he talk like that? Why
can't we talk like real people?


00:38:44.040 --> 00:38:47.589
I am playing in the
manner the Bard intended...


00:38:48.640 --> 00:38:50.870
...in the way the plays have been
performed throughout the ages.


00:38:51.000 --> 00:38:56.677
...by all the great men of our
theatre, Olivier, Gielgud, Richardson.


00:38:56.960 --> 00:38:59.155
How about someone still alive?


00:38:59.520 --> 00:39:02.671
Yeah, okay, I'll do the
directing. Thank you, both.


00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:06.230
Nigel, I think it's important to
remember that Kent is Lear's friend.


00:39:06.360 --> 00:39:07.793
It needs to be warm.


00:39:07.920 --> 00:39:09.478
Oh, God.


00:39:09.600 --> 00:39:12.910
And, Jefferson, I think
that Lear needs a bit more...


00:39:13.480 --> 00:39:15.675
...passion.
- Yes, if only.


00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:19.076
I mean, he's reading it
like a bloody shopping list.


00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:23.912
Nigel? How many
movies have you made?


00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:27.112
How many movies with
your name above the title?


00:39:27.240 --> 00:39:30.596
How many love scenes have you
played with gorgeous starlets?


00:39:30.720 --> 00:39:31.914
I don't think many.


00:39:32.040 --> 00:39:33.155
Bravo!


00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:39.153
<i>The Suffolk Herald hailed
my Malvolio as definitive.</i>


00:39:39.960 --> 00:39:41.439
I rest my case.


00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:44.315
Well, I defer to the director.


00:39:45.080 --> 00:39:49.790
I think there is a definitive
performance lying somewhere between.


00:39:50.520 --> 00:39:56.072
Californian realism and
English mellifluence.


00:39:56.760 --> 00:40:01.311
Yes, well, I expected rather
more support than that, Dorothy.


00:40:01.440 --> 00:40:03.874
I do have a
certain reputation.


00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:05.752
And I'm sorry, but I
won't be treated like this.


00:40:05.880 --> 00:40:08.872
I shall leave while you
reconsider your position.


00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.115
Nigel.


00:40:11.960 --> 00:40:14.076
You're acting like an ass.


00:40:14.200 --> 00:40:18.273
If anybody's gonna be an ass around
here, it's gonna be me. I'm leaving.


00:40:18.400 --> 00:40:20.436
Not before me.


00:40:27.120 --> 00:40:29.714
I can slam the door louder!


00:40:32.080 --> 00:40:33.559
Oh!


00:40:39.480 --> 00:40:40.469
Charlie, listen to me.


00:40:40.600 --> 00:40:45.151
I've got more lines in this play than I
had in my entire motion picture career.


00:40:45.280 --> 00:40:47.077
Yeah, but they're
such good ones.


00:40:47.200 --> 00:40:50.556
<i>Listen, you putz, you didn't
tell me I was doing Lear.</i>


00:40:50.680 --> 00:40:53.148
<i>- I did.
- No, you told me Lear...</i>


00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:56.909
<i>...but you didn't tell
me Shakespeare's Lear!</i>


00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.315
It could be the
end of my career.


00:40:59.440 --> 00:41:02.477
You know what else? I'm
looking out my window at sheep.


00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:05.194
Well, I hear you get a
good leg of lamb out there.


00:41:05.720 --> 00:41:07.438
I'm scared, Charlie.


00:41:07.760 --> 00:41:11.719
Well, that's good. You're...
You're doing scared really well.


00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:13.871
I can't hear you,
it's a bad connection.


00:41:15.120 --> 00:41:17.270
<i>Talk to you a month from now.</i>


00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.872
- Jefferson, it's Dorothy.
- I'm packing.


00:41:32.520 --> 00:41:35.080
We are all so sorry
about last night.


00:41:35.880 --> 00:41:39.509
The whole company is distraught.
No one's talking to Nigel.


00:41:40.320 --> 00:41:41.958
I'm still packing.


00:41:43.680 --> 00:41:45.910
We'd like to
make it up to you.


00:41:47.880 --> 00:41:51.111
- Where are we going?
- Nearly there, Mr Steel.


00:41:52.480 --> 00:41:55.631
Even when the sun is out,
I'm freezing to death.


00:41:55.760 --> 00:41:57.239
Oh, come along.


00:41:58.320 --> 00:41:59.355
Oh.


00:42:01.600 --> 00:42:04.353
Ta-da! Your trailer, sir.


00:42:08.120 --> 00:42:09.553
That's better.


00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:16.991
Your home away
from home, Mr Steel.


00:42:17.120 --> 00:42:20.430
The pub donated the jukebox,
Mary supplied the sofa...


00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:25.039
Kevin provided your minibar, and
Frank provided the television.


00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:28.596
You have to fiddle with the dish a bit.
It's got to point 28 degrees east...


00:42:28.720 --> 00:42:31.439
...which can be a bit
tricky when the wind gets up.


00:42:31.560 --> 00:42:33.278
So, what, no hot tub?


00:42:36.400 --> 00:42:40.188
I've cleaned it out completely.
It shouldn't be whiffy at all.


00:42:43.880 --> 00:42:46.917
You have me for
another rehearsal.


00:42:50.200 --> 00:42:51.792
What did you do
with all the books?


00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:03.309
What happened to
the handsome one?


00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:07.115
Oh!


00:43:08.960 --> 00:43:09.949
Sorry.


00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:15.871
Hey, Amanda! Long time no
see. We should do lunch.


00:43:16.120 --> 00:43:19.032
- That's why I'm here, remember?
- Right.


00:43:20.120 --> 00:43:22.429
So, how's business, Charlie?


00:43:22.840 --> 00:43:25.115
You know, it's up, it's down.


00:43:26.040 --> 00:43:28.315
How about you? You working?


00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:30.596
I just got a call for
an independent movie...


00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:32.398
<i>...a modern dress version
of Sense and Sensibility.</i>


00:43:32.520 --> 00:43:34.476
<i>The director saw me
in Pride and Prejudice.</i>


00:43:34.600 --> 00:43:36.192
Oh, way to go!


00:43:36.760 --> 00:43:40.639
You know, your dad will be thrilled.
He's always been very proud of you.


00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:43.558
Really? He never
mentioned it to me.


00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:45.989
Sorry.


00:43:48.240 --> 00:43:49.229
Flip it.


00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:52.511
- Are you gonna be okay, Charlie?
- I'll be great.


00:43:52.840 --> 00:43:56.913
This is the future, Amanda. All
agents will have offices like this.


00:43:57.040 --> 00:43:59.156
This is better than
the paperless office.


00:43:59.280 --> 00:44:01.555
This is the
office-less office.


00:44:04.320 --> 00:44:06.709
"Come, let's away to prison. "


00:44:07.720 --> 00:44:12.919
"We two alone will sing
like birds in the cage.


00:44:13.880 --> 00:44:16.155
"When thou dost
ask me blessing...


00:44:17.440 --> 00:44:20.113
"I'll kneel down, And
ask of thee forgiveness.


00:44:21.160 --> 00:44:23.720
"So we'll... We'll... "


00:44:25.720 --> 00:44:27.676
"Live, and pray, and sing. "


00:44:28.880 --> 00:44:31.440
"Live, and pray, and sing. "


00:44:31.560 --> 00:44:33.516
Oh, for heaven's sake!


00:44:33.640 --> 00:44:36.074
I mean, are we gonna have
this on the opening night, eh?


00:44:36.200 --> 00:44:39.670
Are we going to have Verity
playing Cordelia and Lear?


00:44:39.800 --> 00:44:44.396
Some of us have a
lot of lines to learn.


00:44:46.280 --> 00:44:48.953
Dorothy, would now be a
good time to talk about.


00:44:49.080 --> 00:44:51.548
...our scene where Gloucester
gets his eyes put out?


00:44:51.680 --> 00:44:53.557
- Yes, okay.
- Okay. Fine.


00:44:54.000 --> 00:44:56.753
"Out, vile jelly! Where
is thy lustre now?"


00:45:03.320 --> 00:45:06.551
- What do you think?
- I think I have to think about it.


00:45:09.960 --> 00:45:16.559
As I was about to say, I would like
everyone word-perfect by Sunday.


00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:17.995
Oh! Sunday?


00:45:19.360 --> 00:45:21.828
And I mean "everyone".


00:45:25.080 --> 00:45:28.470
"Goneril, Our
eldest-born, speak first. "


00:45:29.120 --> 00:45:35.116
"Sir, I do love you more than
word can wield the matter...


00:45:36.200 --> 00:45:40.478
"Dearer than eyesight,
space, and liberty. "


00:45:47.400 --> 00:45:52.110
"Post speedily to my lord your
husband, show him this letter.


00:45:52.240 --> 00:45:54.435
"The army of France is landed.


00:45:54.560 --> 00:45:56.915
"Seek out the
traitor Gloucester. "


00:45:58.240 --> 00:45:59.673
Then you say, "Hang
him instantly".


00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:06.835
"... if this letter speed,
And my invention thrive...


00:46:07.000 --> 00:46:09.798
"Edmund the base Shall
top the legitimate.


00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:16.037
"I grow, I prosper. Now,
gods, stand up for bastards!"


00:46:17.280 --> 00:46:18.633
Kevin Patel.


00:46:19.280 --> 00:46:20.599
Sorry, Mum.


00:46:21.120 --> 00:46:23.953
"'Tis but the
infirmity of his age.


00:46:24.080 --> 00:46:28.039
"Yet he hath ever but
slenderly known himself. "


00:46:28.160 --> 00:46:29.798
Good, good.


00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:35.119
"I yet beseech your majesty, If
for I want that glib and oily art...


00:46:35.240 --> 00:46:36.832
"To speak and purpose not...


00:46:36.960 --> 00:46:41.351
"since what I well intend, I'll do it
before I speak, that you make known.


00:46:41.480 --> 00:46:43.277
"It is no vicious blot.


00:46:43.400 --> 00:46:46.995
"That hath deprived me of
your grace and favour. "


00:46:47.120 --> 00:46:49.236
- Uh-huh. There you
go, Verity. - Oh, great.


00:46:49.360 --> 00:46:51.032
- Thank you,
Ashley. - Bye-bye.


00:46:51.160 --> 00:46:52.513
- See you, Verity.
- Bye, Verity.


00:46:55.920 --> 00:46:59.754
"Now I prithee, daughter,
do not make me mad.


00:46:59.880 --> 00:47:02.838
"I will not trouble
thee, my child.


00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:04.234
"Farewell.


00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:06.838
"We'll no more meet...


00:47:07.920 --> 00:47:12.471
"no more see one another...


00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:18.072
"But yet thou art my flesh,
my blood, my daughter. "


00:47:21.480 --> 00:47:24.836
So, can you eat this or
are you gonna explode?


00:47:24.960 --> 00:47:28.555
I can eat most things.


00:47:28.680 --> 00:47:33.071
Apart from wheat and dairy
products and shellfish and gluten.


00:47:33.200 --> 00:47:37.557
And nuts, obviously, which could
kill me or something equally dramatic.


00:47:41.200 --> 00:47:45.512
- You're meant to feel sorry for me.
- No, you can do that.


00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:50.432
I'll get it.


00:47:52.520 --> 00:47:55.671
I'm so sorry to
disturb you, Dorothy...


00:47:55.800 --> 00:47:59.918
...but I have an announcement to make.
I'm... I'm resigning from the production.


00:48:00.080 --> 00:48:01.354
Not again!


00:48:02.360 --> 00:48:04.191
This time it is a
question of principle.


00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:05.958
Oh, the principle being that
you didn't get the best part?


00:48:06.080 --> 00:48:08.355
- Oh...
- Oh, don't be so pompous, Nigel.


00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:09.469
Come in and have a drink.


00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:13.036
But I am not being pompous.
That appalling American!


00:48:13.720 --> 00:48:17.235
He's an utter disgrace to the
traditions of the amateur stage...


00:48:17.360 --> 00:48:21.592
- To the noble name of The Stratford Players...
- Sounds pompous to me.


00:48:22.840 --> 00:48:24.717
Tell me about your mom.


00:48:25.760 --> 00:48:30.629
Well, my dad left her when I was very young
because he couldn't handle the commitment.


00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:31.829
Mmm.


00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:38.279
She was in the theatre, but then
she gave it up to look after me.


00:48:40.480 --> 00:48:42.471
You know, Dorothy, it
was only for your sake.


00:48:42.600 --> 00:48:47.230
...that I was prepared to humiliate
myself to play the Earl of Kent.


00:48:47.520 --> 00:48:52.719
It's a very difficult part, Nigel.
You're the only one who can do it.


00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:55.077
Do you really think so?


00:48:55.200 --> 00:48:58.909
Do you remember that judge at
the drama festival in Norwich?


00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:02.914
Oh, no, I'm not sure, it
was all such a long time ago.


00:49:03.040 --> 00:49:05.634
One of the best
Malvolio's he'd seen.


00:49:05.760 --> 00:49:09.116
"The best Malvolio ever in the history
of the East Anglia Drama Festival"


00:49:09.240 --> 00:49:11.276
...were, I think,
his exact words.


00:49:11.400 --> 00:49:13.755
And that's why we need you.


00:49:13.880 --> 00:49:18.829
Anyone can play Lear, but how
many people can dazzle as Kent?


00:49:20.280 --> 00:49:22.191
I can think of only one.


00:49:22.440 --> 00:49:25.079
You're not just saying
that to get round me?


00:49:25.200 --> 00:49:26.394
Would I?


00:49:28.400 --> 00:49:29.879
Now, I've got to go.


00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:32.275
"The gods reward
your kindness!"


00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:39.796
I hope everyone's hungry.


00:49:40.720 --> 00:49:42.472
I'm starving.


00:49:45.000 --> 00:49:47.355
Jefferson's taking a breather
from women at the moment.


00:49:47.480 --> 00:49:50.233
- Aren't you, Jefferson?
- Thanks, big mouth.


00:49:50.400 --> 00:49:54.154
Apparently, the only woman
in his life is his daughter.


00:49:54.280 --> 00:49:56.555
She's not talking
to me right now.


00:49:56.680 --> 00:49:59.513
Is this where we're meant
to feel sorry for you?


00:49:59.640 --> 00:50:00.789
Touche.


00:50:02.320 --> 00:50:04.151
- What does your daughter do?
- Oh, Amanda's an actress.


00:50:04.280 --> 00:50:06.271
...and she's really talented.


00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:10.030
There was a time she used
to think I was pretty good.


00:50:10.360 --> 00:50:12.476
- What happened?
- She grew up.


00:50:25.440 --> 00:50:28.398
Is that the brewery
man's wife with Jefferson?


00:50:29.720 --> 00:50:33.429
Oh. Well, I expect she's part
of the sponsorship package...


00:50:33.560 --> 00:50:35.312
...if you get my drift.


00:50:35.800 --> 00:50:39.395
Yes, well, I can't wear Lycra,
it brings me out in a rash.


00:50:40.800 --> 00:50:43.519
Oh, never seen
him quite so perky.


00:50:43.840 --> 00:50:47.116
I wonder how long it will be before
they're working out horizontally.


00:50:47.240 --> 00:50:50.152
- Janine Jarvis, you have a filthy mind.
- Mmm.


00:50:54.560 --> 00:50:57.472
This isn't too much for
you, is it, Mr Steel?


00:50:59.240 --> 00:51:02.198
Of course not. Do I look
like I've had enough?


00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:05.676
No, no, no. Maybe a little.


00:51:05.800 --> 00:51:08.439
- Ready to go again?
- I don't know.


00:51:10.640 --> 00:51:11.709
Hmm.


00:51:15.000 --> 00:51:17.719
- Are you still okay?
- I'm still hanging in there.


00:51:17.840 --> 00:51:18.875
Hi.


00:51:19.000 --> 00:51:19.989
Hello. Morning.


00:51:20.120 --> 00:51:22.509
Mum, you really think
that's such a good idea?


00:51:22.640 --> 00:51:23.595
What?


00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:26.393
Having Lauren as
Jefferson's personal trainer.


00:51:26.520 --> 00:51:27.999
Oh, really, Verity, you
watch too much television.


00:51:28.120 --> 00:51:30.190
I'm just saying.


00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:35.554
- He's not my cup of tea.
- I think you might be his.


00:51:35.680 --> 00:51:38.672
Oh, don't be silly, Verity, he's
too much in love with himself.


00:51:38.800 --> 00:51:41.519
Besides, there is the little
matter of age to consider.


00:51:41.640 --> 00:51:43.995
Yeah, you are older than the
women he usually goes for.


00:51:45.240 --> 00:51:47.754
You are so rude.
Who brought you up?


00:51:48.640 --> 00:51:51.632
I spend too much time already
pandering to that insufferable ego.


00:51:51.760 --> 00:51:53.478
It's not his fault.


00:51:53.600 --> 00:51:57.878
If you're treated like a king,
you're gonna end up behaving like one.


00:51:59.360 --> 00:52:01.999
I think his heart's
in the right place.


00:52:03.200 --> 00:52:04.872
Let's just hope it holds
out till the opening night.


00:52:06.080 --> 00:52:08.230
Oh, that... Ooh! Ooh! There?


00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:09.395
Yeah, right there.


00:52:09.520 --> 00:52:11.909
That's it, that's the spot.
You got a big one here.


00:52:16.920 --> 00:52:19.559
Oh, you're so stiff
in there, Mr Steel.


00:52:20.040 --> 00:52:24.352
Yeah. Oh, a little
harder. A little harder.


00:52:25.000 --> 00:52:26.718
And there they were...


00:52:26.840 --> 00:52:32.836
Jefferson Steel and the brewer's wife
in his bedroom, at it like knives.


00:52:32.960 --> 00:52:35.110
I think it's disgusting.


00:52:35.240 --> 00:52:37.993
Well, I'm shocked and disappointed,
but I'm hardly surprised.


00:52:38.120 --> 00:52:40.190
Okay, everyone. Here we go.


00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:42.550
Let's pick it up
from Act 1, Scene 3.


00:52:42.680 --> 00:52:44.716
King Lear is dividing his
time between his two daughters.


00:52:44.840 --> 00:52:46.558
...and neither is happy with
the domestic arrangements.


00:52:46.680 --> 00:52:49.069
Yes, I know exactly
how she feels.


00:52:50.240 --> 00:52:51.559
Okay, Mary?


00:52:53.360 --> 00:52:55.874
"By day and night
he wrongs me.


00:52:56.000 --> 00:52:58.798
"Every hour He flashes into
one gross crime or other.


00:52:58.920 --> 00:53:01.832
"That sets us all at
odds. I'll not endure it!"


00:53:02.240 --> 00:53:05.550
That's good, Mary, I can
really feel your anger.


00:53:18.160 --> 00:53:19.673
I like to think of
myself as broad-minded...


00:53:19.800 --> 00:53:23.588
...but I am not having that sort
of thing going on under my roof.


00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:25.872
What sort of thing?


00:53:26.040 --> 00:53:30.909
Goodbye, Mr Steel. You have
been a huge disappointment to me.


00:53:33.800 --> 00:53:35.791
Where am I gonna sleep?


00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:39.749
You can sleep with
one of your floozies!


00:53:46.080 --> 00:53:47.752
You never did
like me, did you?


00:53:58.760 --> 00:54:00.671
Flint Farm. Bed and breakfast.


00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:04.156
Jefferson Steel, please.
This is his daughter, Amanda.


00:54:04.280 --> 00:54:06.475
Mr Steel is no longer
staying at this establishment.


00:54:06.600 --> 00:54:10.229
We do try to uphold certain
moral standards here, you know.


00:54:11.240 --> 00:54:14.391
Excuse me? Do you know
where I can reach him?


00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:18.593
No, I have no idea where he is and
nor do I wish to know. Good night.


00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:19.955
Hello?


00:54:20.840 --> 00:54:21.829
Hello?


00:54:36.040 --> 00:54:37.359
Well, thank you very much.


00:54:37.480 --> 00:54:40.278
I've just lost my Goneril.
Mary is out of the play.


00:54:40.400 --> 00:54:42.755
Good. She's out of her mind.


00:54:42.880 --> 00:54:45.633
And I know all
about you and Lauren!


00:54:46.520 --> 00:54:50.433
Nothing happened. You
don't know anything.


00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:54.270
I know that, thanks to you, I've just
lost a rather important member of my cast.


00:54:54.400 --> 00:54:55.469
Yeah.


00:54:56.440 --> 00:54:58.590
Me. I quit.


00:54:59.720 --> 00:55:01.950
- I'm going home.
- I've been waiting for this.


00:55:02.080 --> 00:55:05.516
A celebrity tantrum. Well,
maybe it's all for the best.


00:55:05.800 --> 00:55:07.756
No, that's not your line.


00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:09.871
You're supposed to say,
"Please, Jefferson, stay. "


00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:12.639
No, no, no. No, I think
you're doing the right thing.


00:55:12.760 --> 00:55:15.752
<i>I mean, obviously Lear is just
too difficult for a film actor.</i>


00:55:15.880 --> 00:55:17.598
You can't cope
with the verse...


00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:20.280
...and it is an awful lot of
